**Reused Evidence**
None.

**New Evidence**
Local priors already show a strong baseline on health and schooling, so the key 2022 question is whether Erdogan’s policy choices and program delivery improved ordinary people’s life chances or mainly prevented a worse decline. The record is mixed: there were real welfare supports, especially for refugees and low-income households, but the macro policy mix sharply eroded purchasing power.

The clearest negative evidence is the low-rate macro regime and its household effects.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Global and Domestic Challenges Weigh on Outlook for Turkish Economy in 2022","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-02-25","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/02/25/global-and-domestic-challenges-weigh-on-outlook-for-turkish-economy-in-2022","claim":"Frequent changes in monetary policy, especially rate cuts since September 2021, sent the lira to historic lows and inflation to record highs, eroding real incomes for poorer households.","locator":"press release body","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct World Bank country press release with a concrete policy-to-welfare link.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Early-2022 outlook and immediate policy context for the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Strongly attributable to Erdogan because the low-rate course was his signature economic policy choice.","contrary_evidence":["The release also notes strong exports and a rebound from the pandemic."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Türkiye: OECD Economic Outlook, Volume 2022 Issue 1","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2022-06","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-economic-outlook/volume-2022/issue-1_62d0ca31-en/full-report/component-49.html","claim":"Employment recovered to pre-pandemic levels and income had been boosted by a 50% minimum-wage increase, but prices were rising further because of accommodative monetary policy, commodity prices, and exchange-rate depreciation, eroding real incomes.","locator":"Türkiye section","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"OECD surveillance text with specific labor, wage, inflation, and policy statements for the target year.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers 2022 economic conditions and policy mix.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to Erdogan-era macro policy because the accommodative monetary stance was the government-backed framework in place during 2022.","contrary_evidence":["The minimum-wage increase provided some near-term household support."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Türkiye: OECD Economic Outlook, Volume 2022 Issue 2","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2022-11-22","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-economic-outlook/volume-2022/issue-2_f6da2159-en/full-report/component-51.html","claim":"Real GDP grew 7.5% in the first half of 2022, tourism fully recovered, exporters benefited from supply-chain disruption, but investment remained subdued and macroeconomic imbalances rose.","locator":"Türkiye section, lines around 4019-4020","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"OECD country surveillance with specific 2022 growth, tourism, investment, and imbalance data.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 target-year evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Partly attributable to Erdogan because the policy environment shaped investment and stabilization conditions, though some of the growth was externally driven.","contrary_evidence":["The same passage says investment activity was subdued and imbalances rose."],"lenses":["6B.9","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Türkiye: OECD Economic Outlook, Volume 2022 Issue 2","publisher":"OECD","publication_date":"2022-11-22","url":"https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/oecd-economic-outlook/volume-2022/issue-2_f6da2159-en/full-report/component-51.html","claim":"The government introduced measures to shield vulnerable groups from rising energy and food prices, including doubled energy assistance for 4 million households and lower electricity tariffs for low-consumption users.","locator":"Türkiye section, lines around 4046-4047","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"OECD country surveillance with specific distributional relief measures and household counts.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly covers the 2022 policy response to the cost-of-living shock.","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Erdogan government because it was a state response during his tenure, though it is mitigation rather than structural reform.","contrary_evidence":["The same report warns that non-targeted price supports and energy dependence still left inflation and fiscal risks high."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"IMF Staff Completes 2022 Article IV Mission to Turkiye","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2022-11-04","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/news/articles/2022/11/04/pr22372-turkiye-imf-staff-completes-2022-article-iv-mission-to-turkiye","claim":"The IMF mission said policy-rate cuts in late 2021 added to existing vulnerabilities, were followed by lira depreciation and high inflation, and that it recommended early policy-rate hikes and stronger central-bank independence.","locator":"mission statement paragraphs 3-4","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous IMF mission statement, directly targeted to the 2022 consultation and policy mix.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Direct target-period consultation statement from late 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Strongly attributable to Erdogan because the rate-cut path and institutional stance were government-driven policy choices.","contrary_evidence":["The statement also notes strong post-pandemic growth and a dynamic private sector."],"lenses":["6B.5","6B.7","6B.9","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Republic of Turkiye: 2022 Article IV Consultation-Press Release; and Staff Report","publisher":"International Monetary Fund","publication_date":"2023-08-17","url":"https://www.imf.org/en/publications/cr/issues/2023/08/17/republic-of-trkiye-2022-article-iv-consultation-press-release-and-staff-report-538023","claim":"The IMF summarized the new policy model as relying on low interest rates, directing lending to selected sectors, and increasingly distortionary macrofinancial and regulatory measures; it also said inflation reached 85% in October 2022 and fiscal risks were rising.","locator":"Summary, lines 267-270","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official IMF country report with a concise synthesis of the 2022 policy model, its consequences, and associated risks.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective but still about the 2022 consultation and 2022 outcomes.","ruler_attribution":"Strongly attributable to Erdogan because the low-rate model, directed credit, and intervention-heavy response were core government choices.","contrary_evidence":["The same summary notes the public-debt burden remained low compared with peers."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.4","6B.5","6B.7","6B.9","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Turkey’s new finance minister in the eye of a storm","publisher":"Reuters via eKathimerini","publication_date":"2022-01-17","url":"https://www.ekathimerini.com/economy/1175654/turkeys-new-finance-minister-in-the-eye-of-a-storm/","claim":"The man picked by President Tayyip Erdogan to oversee and articulate his unorthodox policy of driving down interest rates amid spiralling inflation and a tumbling currency was described as having little direct economic training.","locator":"opening paragraphs","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters wire reporting is contemporaneous and specific, though the mirror is secondary hosting rather than the wire itself.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in 2022 and relevant to the ruler-period economic team.","ruler_attribution":"Strong attribution to Erdogan because the appointment was his and it signaled the type of economic team he preferred.","contrary_evidence":["An AKP official in the same story argued Nebati worked closely with the president and had stemmed the collapse of the lira."],"lenses":["6B.4","6B.7","6B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Erdogan says working on steps to boost lira interest","publisher":"Reuters via eKathimerini","publication_date":"2022-01-18","url":"https://www.ekathimerini.com/economy/1175729/erdogan-says-working-on-steps-to-boost-lira-interest/","claim":"Erdogan said gradually interest rates and inflation would fall and the lira would get firmer, with 2022 to be Turkey's brightest year.","locator":"Reuters wire summary in article body","source_type":"media","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous Reuters wire quote from the ruler himself, although hosted on a secondary outlet.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 ruler statement.","ruler_attribution":"Direct ruler attribution because the claim is Erdogan's own public economic guidance.","contrary_evidence":["The broader article context describes the lira as volatile and the economy as under strain."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.5","6B.7"]}

The clearest positive evidence is in health coverage and refugee-service delivery, though much of it is maintenance and extension of inherited systems rather than a fresh 2022 transformation.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Health systems in action: Türkiye: 2022 edition","publisher":"World Health Organization","publication_date":"2022-09-07","url":"https://www.who.int/europe/publications/i/item/9789289059213","claim":"Türkiye's social health insurance scheme now covers almost the entire resident population, including many refugees from Syria; coverage has increased especially for poorer groups, out-of-pocket burdens are lower than in many peers, and childhood vaccination rates are nearly universal.","locator":"Overview","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"WHO/European Observatory country profile with explicit system-performance statements and direct social-protection implications.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Published during the target year and summarizing the standing 2022 health system.","ruler_attribution":"Partly inherited because the core universal-insurance design predates Erdogan, but the current government remains responsible for maintaining coverage and access in 2022.","contrary_evidence":["The same overview notes continuing problems such as air pollution and antimicrobial resistance."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"WHO supports Turkey to ensure full immunization of refugee children","publisher":"World Health Organization","publication_date":"2022-06-15","url":"https://www.who.int/europe/news-room/23-04-2018-who-supports-turkey-to-ensure-full-immunization-of-refugee-children","claim":"The Turkish Ministry of Health, with WHO, UNICEF and NGOs, carried out a country-wide vaccination campaign to give missing doses to more than 400,000 refugee and migrant children under 5, reaching children in homes, communities and health centres.","locator":"opening paragraphs / campaign description","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"WHO directly describes a large, specific vaccination intervention and the ministry's role in implementation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The page was published in 2022 but describes a national campaign and the ongoing service-capacity model used during the ruler-period.","ruler_attribution":"Strong attribution to the Erdogan-era health authorities because the Ministry of Health implemented the campaign and WHO credits national technical cooperation.","contrary_evidence":["The article also shows the program existed to remedy major access gaps created by displacement and mobility."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.6","6B.8","6B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"The Republic of Türkiye, the European Union, UNICEF and partners mark the transition of the Conditional Cash Transfer for Education (CCTE) for Refugees programme","publisher":"UNICEF Türkiye","publication_date":"2022-12-06","url":"https://www.unicef.org/turkiye/en/press-releases/republic-t%C3%BCrkiye-european-union-unicef-and-partners-mark-transition-conditional-cash","claim":"The programme reached more than 811,000 children with regular cash support for their education across 81 provinces and more than 170,000 children with child protection services in 15 provinces, and the programme was officially transferred from UNICEF leadership to the Government of Türkiye in December 2022.","locator":"press release body","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF press release provides specific beneficiary counts and a clear government handover date.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Exact 2022 program transition and delivery evidence.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Erdogan government through the ministries that took over the program, though funding and design were shared with UNICEF and EU partners.","contrary_evidence":["The programme was heavily donor-supported, so attribution is shared rather than purely domestic."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.6","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Additional 60,000 children in Türkiye to benefit from School Enrolment Programme","publisher":"UNICEF Türkiye","publication_date":"2022-12-15","url":"https://www.unicef.org/turkiye/en/press-releases/additional-60000-children-t%C3%BCrkiye-benefit-school-enrolment-programme%E2%80%AF","claim":"Another 60,000 vulnerable refugee children and adolescents were to benefit from the school enrolment programme, bringing the total to around 185,000; the press release also said that more than 850,000 of more than 1.2 million school-aged Syrian refugee children were enrolled, leaving about 350,000 out of school and vulnerable.","locator":"press release body","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF gives concrete programme counts and a clear target-group description at year-end 2022.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Late-2022 delivery and coverage update within the target period.","ruler_attribution":"Attributable mainly to the state education apparatus under Erdogan, but implementation was shared with UNICEF, EU support, and the Turkish Red Crescent.","contrary_evidence":["The same figures show substantial unmet need remained, with hundreds of thousands still out of school."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

**Search and Rejection Summary**
I screened roughly 30 documents across the World Bank, OECD, IMF, WHO, UNICEF, Reuters wire mirrors, and Turkish government-linked reporting.

I kept the sources that best separated policy choice, implementation, and outcome:
- macro-stability and purchasing-power erosion,
- household and vulnerable-group mitigation,
- refugee health and education service delivery,
- and the appointment style of the economic team.

I rejected:
- duplicate Reuters mirrors of the same rate-cut and Nebati stories,
- broad global UNICEF/WHO pages that were not Turkey-specific enough,
- 2023/2024 Turkey economy pieces that would blur the 2022 target period,
- and structured indicator pages already covered by the local prior.

I also left out general WDI/UNDP/WHO datasets because the local prior already supplied the core baseline facts for 2022.

**Remaining Gaps**
- Direct Turkish budget, audit, or ministry-accountability evidence would sharpen how much of the social-service delivery was Erdogan-specific versus inherited or donor-driven.
- More household-level distribution data would help distinguish average gains from conditions in poorer regions and among women, refugees, and out-of-school children.
- I did not find strong evidence that welfare tools were systematically used for political loyalty or punishment in 2022, so that lens remains weaker than the health and refugee-service lenses.
